<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Tanukhids</h1><p class="st-meta">270 CE – 400 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Arab tribal confederation that served Rome as allied frontier troops in Syria, remembered for Queen Mavia's revolt against the emperor Valens.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Arabic tradition says the Tanukh were a coalition of tribes that migrated from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, where some settled around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hira">al-Hira</a>, while others moved into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> in the later 3rd century. There they became foederati, allies who guarded the desert frontier for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Tanukhids were pastoral and semi-settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> who adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> during the 4th century. Their best-known leader is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mavia">Mavia</a>, who around 378 led a revolt that ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> and ended only when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> agreed to her demand for an orthodox Arab hermit, Moses, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of her people. Her troops then helped defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adrianople">battle of Adrianople</a>. Little else about their organisation is recorded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 5th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salihids">Salihids</a> replaced them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> leading Arab allies. Tanukh clans remained in northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinnasrin">Qinnasrin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after the Arab conquest, and later Tanukhid families played a part in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tanukhids.jpg" alt="Tanukhids" width="320" height="200"><figcaption>Image: MWahaiibii · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanukh_Map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>270 CE – 400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mavia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tanukh, Tanukhid confederation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=335&amp;focus=tanukhids" aria-label="Tanukhids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5665 917 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tanukhids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5985 1173h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5985 1173h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Syria. <a href="/atlas?year=335&amp;focus=tanukhids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
